1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,842
When people picture a nuclear power plant,

2
00:00:02,842 --> 00:00:05,753
they picture giant curved towers with white

3
00:00:05,753 --> 00:00:07,581
clouds pouring out of them.

4
00:00:07,761 --> 00:00:10,932
Most people think those clouds are smoke,

5
00:00:10,932 --> 00:00:12,324
or even radiation.

6
00:00:12,504 --> 00:00:13,339
They are neither.

7
00:00:13,519 --> 00:00:15,239
They are water vapor,

8
00:00:15,239 --> 00:00:17,532
the same stuff as a cloud in

9
00:00:17,532 --> 00:00:18,187
the sky.

10
00:00:18,367 --> 00:00:20,420
And the towers are not even the

11
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:21,281
nuclear part.

12
00:00:21,461 --> 00:00:24,664
Today, eleven parts of a nuclear power

13
00:00:24,664 --> 00:00:27,277
plant, and why each one exists,

14
00:00:27,277 --> 00:00:29,553
starting with those towers.

15
00:00:29,902 --> 00:00:31,563
A nuclear plant is,

16
00:00:31,563 --> 00:00:32,525
in the end,

17
00:00:32,525 --> 00:00:34,535
a way of boiling water.

18
00:00:34,715 --> 00:00:37,861
Uranium atoms split and release heat.

19
00:00:38,041 --> 00:00:39,795
That heat makes steam,

20
00:00:39,795 --> 00:00:41,868
the steam spins a turbine,

21
00:00:41,868 --> 00:00:44,659
and the turbine drives a generator.

22
00:00:44,839 --> 00:00:46,564
Most of the engineering,

23
00:00:46,564 --> 00:00:48,719
and most of the parts you see,

24
00:00:48,719 --> 00:00:51,163
exist to control that heat safely,

25
00:00:51,163 --> 00:00:53,031
and to get rid of the heat

26
00:00:53,031 --> 00:00:55,834
that cannot be turned into electricity.

27
00:00:56,183 --> 00:00:59,213
As always, every fact comes from a

28
00:00:59,213 --> 00:01:02,153
named source, nuclear regulators,

29
00:01:02,153 --> 00:01:06,340
plant operators, and official accident reports,

30
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:09,013
all listed in the description.

31
00:01:09,193 --> 00:01:11,224
Let's start with the towers.

32
00:01:11,575 --> 00:01:14,434
Why do nuclear plants have those giant

33
00:01:14,434 --> 00:01:15,487
curved towers?

34
00:01:15,667 --> 00:01:18,236
Because a power plant must throw away

35
00:01:18,236 --> 00:01:19,208
a lot of heat.

36
00:01:19,388 --> 00:01:22,034
After the steam spins the turbine,

37
00:01:22,034 --> 00:01:24,290
it has to be cooled back into

38
00:01:24,290 --> 00:01:27,169
water, and that takes huge amounts of

39
00:01:27,169 --> 00:01:28,258
cooling water.

40
00:01:28,438 --> 00:01:30,783
The tower cools that water down.

41
00:01:30,963 --> 00:01:33,970
Warm water is sprayed inside near the

42
00:01:33,970 --> 00:01:36,571
bottom, air flows up through it,

43
00:01:36,571 --> 00:01:39,253
and some of the water evaporates,

44
00:01:39,253 --> 00:01:41,122
carrying the heat away.

45
00:01:41,472 --> 00:01:43,968
The tower works like a chimney with

46
00:01:43,968 --> 00:01:44,467
no fan.

47
00:01:44,647 --> 00:01:47,361
The warm, moist air inside is lighter

48
00:01:47,361 --> 00:01:48,901
than the air outside,

49
00:01:48,901 --> 00:01:49,782
so it rises,

50
00:01:49,782 --> 00:01:51,909
and fresh air is pulled in at

51
00:01:51,909 --> 00:01:52,716
the bottom.

52
00:01:52,896 --> 00:01:54,688
A tower can be more than a

53
00:01:54,688 --> 00:01:56,756
hundred and fifty meters tall,

54
00:01:56,756 --> 00:01:58,479
because the taller it is,

55
00:01:58,479 --> 00:02:00,616
the stronger the natural draft.

56
00:02:00,967 --> 00:02:03,027
The white cloud at the top is

57
00:02:03,027 --> 00:02:05,798
just water vapor condensing in the cold

58
00:02:05,798 --> 00:02:08,142
air, like your breath on a winter

59
00:02:08,142 --> 00:02:08,710
morning.

60
00:02:08,890 --> 00:02:12,114
The water inside the tower never touches

61
00:02:12,114 --> 00:02:13,082
the reactor.

62
00:02:13,262 --> 00:02:14,957
It is in a separate loop.

63
00:02:15,137 --> 00:02:17,622
That is also why coal and gas

64
00:02:17,622 --> 00:02:21,220
plants often have exactly the same towers.

65
00:02:21,571 --> 00:02:24,005
Why are cooling towers wide at the

66
00:02:24,005 --> 00:02:26,081
bottom, narrow in the middle,

67
00:02:26,081 --> 00:02:28,014
and wider again at the top?

68
00:02:28,194 --> 00:02:30,704
The shape is called a hyperboloid,

69
00:02:30,704 --> 00:02:32,919
and it is surprisingly strong.

70
00:02:33,099 --> 00:02:35,712
It can be built from straight lines.

71
00:02:35,892 --> 00:02:39,579
Imagine many straight steel beams leaning in

72
00:02:39,579 --> 00:02:42,428
a circle, all tilted the same way.

73
00:02:42,608 --> 00:02:45,271
Together, they make this curved shape,

74
00:02:45,271 --> 00:02:47,863
which resists wind and its own weight

75
00:02:47,863 --> 00:02:48,564
very well.

76
00:02:48,914 --> 00:02:50,788
Because it is so strong,

77
00:02:50,788 --> 00:02:53,599
the concrete shell can be very thin,

78
00:02:53,599 --> 00:02:56,488
often only around twenty centimeters,

79
00:02:56,488 --> 00:02:59,065
for a tower over a hundred meters

80
00:02:59,065 --> 00:02:59,455
tall.

81
00:02:59,635 --> 00:03:00,881
That is thinner,

82
00:03:00,881 --> 00:03:02,515
relative to its size,

83
00:03:02,515 --> 00:03:03,838
than an eggshell.

84
00:03:04,018 --> 00:03:06,747
Less concrete means a cheaper tower.

85
00:03:07,097 --> 00:03:09,256
The shape also helps the air.

86
00:03:09,436 --> 00:03:11,873
The wide base gives room for the

87
00:03:11,873 --> 00:03:14,386
water sprays and lets lots of air

88
00:03:14,386 --> 00:03:14,614
in.

89
00:03:14,794 --> 00:03:17,580
The narrow waist speeds up the rising

90
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:20,064
air, and the flared top helps the

91
00:03:20,064 --> 00:03:22,399
plume mix with the outside air.

92
00:03:22,579 --> 00:03:25,686
One shape, solving the structure and the

93
00:03:25,686 --> 00:03:27,629
airflow at the same time.

94
00:03:27,979 --> 00:03:30,347
Why do some nuclear plants have no

95
00:03:30,347 --> 00:03:31,879
cooling towers at all?

96
00:03:32,059 --> 00:03:34,350
Because they use the sea,

97
00:03:34,350 --> 00:03:37,098
a lake or a big river instead.

98
00:03:37,278 --> 00:03:39,828
They pump huge amounts of water in,

99
00:03:39,828 --> 00:03:42,524
pass it through the condenser to cool

100
00:03:42,524 --> 00:03:44,636
the steam, and send it back a

101
00:03:44,636 --> 00:03:46,020
few degrees warmer.

102
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:47,912
Plants on the coast,

103
00:03:47,912 --> 00:03:49,623
like many in France,

104
00:03:49,623 --> 00:03:52,105
Japan and the United Kingdom,

105
00:03:52,105 --> 00:03:53,817
often work this way.

106
00:03:54,166 --> 00:03:56,728
It is cheaper and more efficient,

107
00:03:56,728 --> 00:03:58,125
but it has limits.

108
00:03:58,305 --> 00:03:59,924
In very hot summers,

109
00:03:59,924 --> 00:04:02,433
river water can get too warm to

110
00:04:02,433 --> 00:04:04,376
cool the plant properly,

111
00:04:04,376 --> 00:04:08,018
and environmental rules limit how much warmer

112
00:04:08,018 --> 00:04:09,718
the river may become.

113
00:04:09,898 --> 00:04:12,488
Some plants in Europe have had to

114
00:04:12,488 --> 00:04:15,783
reduce power during heat waves for exactly

115
00:04:15,783 --> 00:04:16,725
this reason.

116
00:04:17,074 --> 00:04:20,582
Sea intakes have their own surprise enemy,

117
00:04:20,582 --> 00:04:21,417
jellyfish.

118
00:04:21,597 --> 00:04:24,891
Large swarms have been sucked into intake

119
00:04:24,891 --> 00:04:26,899
screens and clogged them,

120
00:04:26,899 --> 00:04:29,069
forcing plants in Scotland,

121
00:04:29,069 --> 00:04:32,041
Sweden and elsewhere to shut down for

122
00:04:32,041 --> 00:04:32,684
a while.

123
00:04:32,864 --> 00:04:34,654
A nuclear reactor,

124
00:04:34,654 --> 00:04:36,742
stopped by jellyfish.

125
00:04:37,091 --> 00:04:40,019
Why does the reactor sit inside a

126
00:04:40,019 --> 00:04:42,769
huge concrete dome or cylinder?

127
00:04:42,949 --> 00:04:45,274
It is the containment building,

128
00:04:45,274 --> 00:04:48,274
the last barrier between the reactor and

129
00:04:48,274 --> 00:04:49,624
the outside world.

130
00:04:49,804 --> 00:04:52,740
Its walls are reinforced concrete,

131
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:55,071
often around a meter thick,

132
00:04:55,071 --> 00:04:56,538
lined with steel.

133
00:04:56,718 --> 00:04:58,852
If something breaks inside,

134
00:04:58,852 --> 00:05:02,407
and radioactive steam escapes from the pipes,

135
00:05:02,407 --> 00:05:05,409
the containment is designed to hold it

136
00:05:05,409 --> 00:05:05,646
in.

137
00:05:05,996 --> 00:05:09,264
The round shape is strong against pressure

138
00:05:09,264 --> 00:05:11,753
from inside, like a balloon or a

139
00:05:11,753 --> 00:05:12,997
pressure cooker.

140
00:05:13,177 --> 00:05:16,389
It also resists things from outside,

141
00:05:16,389 --> 00:05:19,422
like storms, earthquakes and even,

142
00:05:19,422 --> 00:05:20,849
in many designs,

143
00:05:20,849 --> 00:05:23,882
the impact of a crashing airplane.

144
00:05:24,231 --> 00:05:26,442
In nineteen seventy nine,

145
00:05:26,442 --> 00:05:29,714
at Three Mile Island in Pennsylvania,

146
00:05:29,714 --> 00:05:32,544
part of the reactor core melted.

147
00:05:32,724 --> 00:05:35,992
But the containment building did its job,

148
00:05:35,992 --> 00:05:39,818
and only small amounts of radioactivity escaped.

149
00:05:39,998 --> 00:05:42,709
At Chernobyl in nineteen eighty six,

150
00:05:42,709 --> 00:05:45,571
the reactor had no containment of this

151
00:05:45,571 --> 00:05:49,336
kind, and the explosion threw radioactive material

152
00:05:49,336 --> 00:05:50,993
straight into the sky.

153
00:05:51,342 --> 00:05:53,590
How do you turn off a nuclear

154
00:05:53,590 --> 00:05:54,210
reactor?

155
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:56,225
You push rods into it.

156
00:05:56,405 --> 00:05:59,066
Uranium splits when it is hit by

157
00:05:59,066 --> 00:06:03,059
neutrons, and each split releases more neutrons,

158
00:06:03,059 --> 00:06:04,973
which split more atoms.

159
00:06:05,153 --> 00:06:08,462
Control rods are made of materials like

160
00:06:08,462 --> 00:06:11,770
boron or cadmium that soak up neutrons.

161
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:13,199
Push them in,

162
00:06:13,199 --> 00:06:16,657
and the chain reaction slows down or

163
00:06:16,657 --> 00:06:17,233
stops.

164
00:06:17,413 --> 00:06:18,975
Pull them out a little,

165
00:06:18,975 --> 00:06:20,130
and it speeds up.

166
00:06:20,479 --> 00:06:21,923
In an emergency,

167
00:06:21,923 --> 00:06:24,539
all the rods drop in at once,

168
00:06:24,539 --> 00:06:27,155
often in a couple of seconds.

169
00:06:27,335 --> 00:06:29,054
This is called a scram.

170
00:06:29,234 --> 00:06:30,693
In many reactors,

171
00:06:30,693 --> 00:06:34,041
the rods are held up by electromagnets,

172
00:06:34,041 --> 00:06:35,930
so if the power fails,

173
00:06:35,930 --> 00:06:38,591
they simply fall in by gravity.

174
00:06:38,771 --> 00:06:40,952
The safe position is the one they

175
00:06:40,952 --> 00:06:42,208
reach on their own.

176
00:06:42,558 --> 00:06:45,520
But stopping the chain reaction does not

177
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:46,853
stop all the heat.

178
00:06:47,033 --> 00:06:50,664
The radioactive fuel keeps producing heat for

179
00:06:50,664 --> 00:06:51,874
days and weeks,

180
00:06:51,874 --> 00:06:55,021
at first several percent of full power.

181
00:06:55,201 --> 00:06:57,818
That is why a stopped reactor still

182
00:06:57,818 --> 00:07:00,959
needs cooling, and why losing that cooling

183
00:07:00,959 --> 00:07:02,155
is so dangerous.

184
00:07:02,505 --> 00:07:04,827
How much energy is in nuclear fuel?

185
00:07:05,007 --> 00:07:08,137
It comes as small ceramic pellets of

186
00:07:08,137 --> 00:07:11,093
uranium oxide, about the size of a

187
00:07:11,093 --> 00:07:11,962
fingertip.

188
00:07:12,142 --> 00:07:15,119
According to the nuclear industry,

189
00:07:15,119 --> 00:07:18,271
one pellet can produce about as much

190
00:07:18,271 --> 00:07:20,547
energy as a tonne of coal.

191
00:07:20,727 --> 00:07:24,072
Splitting an atom releases millions of times

192
00:07:24,072 --> 00:07:26,277
more energy than burning one.

193
00:07:26,626 --> 00:07:29,968
The pellets are stacked inside long metal

194
00:07:29,968 --> 00:07:31,924
tubes, called fuel rods,

195
00:07:31,924 --> 00:07:34,858
and the rods are grouped into square

196
00:07:34,858 --> 00:07:37,384
bundles called fuel assemblies,

197
00:07:37,384 --> 00:07:39,014
several meters long.

198
00:07:39,194 --> 00:07:41,800
A large reactor holds a few hundred

199
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:44,630
assemblies, and around a third of them

200
00:07:44,630 --> 00:07:46,938
are replaced every year or two.

201
00:07:47,288 --> 00:07:49,708
The pellets are made of ceramic for

202
00:07:49,708 --> 00:07:50,330
a reason.

203
00:07:50,510 --> 00:07:54,248
Ceramic stays solid at very high temperatures

204
00:07:54,248 --> 00:07:57,737
and traps most of the radioactive products

205
00:07:57,737 --> 00:07:58,568
inside it.

206
00:07:58,748 --> 00:08:01,025
The metal tube around it is another

207
00:08:01,025 --> 00:08:01,546
barrier.

208
00:08:01,726 --> 00:08:04,162
Nuclear safety is built in layers,

209
00:08:04,162 --> 00:08:06,814
starting with a pellet you could hold

210
00:08:06,814 --> 00:08:07,960
in your fingers.

211
00:08:08,310 --> 00:08:11,132
Why does the water around nuclear fuel

212
00:08:11,132 --> 00:08:12,618
sometimes glow blue?

213
00:08:12,798 --> 00:08:15,723
It is called Cherenkov radiation.

214
00:08:15,903 --> 00:08:18,900
Particles from the fuel travel through the

215
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:21,825
water faster than light travels in water.

216
00:08:22,005 --> 00:08:24,072
They do not break the speed of

217
00:08:24,072 --> 00:08:25,312
light in a vacuum,

218
00:08:25,312 --> 00:08:26,208
but in water,

219
00:08:26,208 --> 00:08:27,310
light is slower.

220
00:08:27,490 --> 00:08:29,705
The result is a kind of optical

221
00:08:29,705 --> 00:08:31,849
shock wave, like a sonic boom,

222
00:08:31,849 --> 00:08:33,493
but made of blue light.

223
00:08:33,842 --> 00:08:36,403
Used fuel is stored in deep pools

224
00:08:36,403 --> 00:08:39,041
of water for years after it leaves

225
00:08:39,041 --> 00:08:39,973
the reactor.

226
00:08:40,153 --> 00:08:42,219
The water does two jobs.

227
00:08:42,399 --> 00:08:43,873
It cools the fuel,

228
00:08:43,873 --> 00:08:46,329
which is still producing heat,

229
00:08:46,329 --> 00:08:48,622
and it blocks the radiation.

230
00:08:48,802 --> 00:08:50,973
A few meters of water above the

231
00:08:50,973 --> 00:08:53,214
fuel make it safe for workers to

232
00:08:53,214 --> 00:08:55,315
stand at the edge of the pool.

233
00:08:55,665 --> 00:08:57,259
After several years,

234
00:08:57,259 --> 00:08:59,649
when the fuel has cooled down,

235
00:08:59,649 --> 00:09:02,119
it can be moved into dry casks,

236
00:09:02,119 --> 00:09:05,545
thick containers of steel and concrete that

237
00:09:05,545 --> 00:09:07,856
sit outside on concrete pads.

238
00:09:08,036 --> 00:09:10,440
A pool of water and a steel

239
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:14,092
can, keeping some of the most radioactive

240
00:09:14,092 --> 00:09:16,140
material on Earth safe.

241
00:09:16,489 --> 00:09:19,935
Why is a nuclear reactor core held

242
00:09:19,935 --> 00:09:22,469
inside a giant steel pot?

243
00:09:22,649 --> 00:09:24,750
It is the reactor pressure vessel.

244
00:09:24,930 --> 00:09:27,424
In the most common type of reactor,

245
00:09:27,424 --> 00:09:29,918
the water around the core is heated

246
00:09:29,918 --> 00:09:32,198
to around three hundred degrees,

247
00:09:32,198 --> 00:09:35,262
but kept from boiling by enormous pressure,

248
00:09:35,262 --> 00:09:37,756
more than a hundred and fifty times

249
00:09:37,756 --> 00:09:39,466
the pressure of the air.

250
00:09:39,646 --> 00:09:43,016
Holding that takes steel walls around twenty

251
00:09:43,016 --> 00:09:44,394
centimeters thick.

252
00:09:44,744 --> 00:09:47,959
The hot pressurized water then flows to

253
00:09:47,959 --> 00:09:51,338
steam generators, where it heats a second

254
00:09:51,338 --> 00:09:54,223
loop of water that boils into steam

255
00:09:54,223 --> 00:09:55,542
for the turbine.

256
00:09:55,722 --> 00:09:57,963
The water that touches the fuel never

257
00:09:57,963 --> 00:09:59,356
leaves the containment.

258
00:09:59,536 --> 00:10:03,662
That separation keeps radioactivity away from the

259
00:10:03,662 --> 00:10:06,188
turbine and the outside world.

260
00:10:06,539 --> 00:10:09,325
The pressure vessel cannot be replaced.

261
00:10:09,505 --> 00:10:13,063
Over decades, neutrons slowly make its steel

262
00:10:13,063 --> 00:10:14,114
more brittle.

263
00:10:14,294 --> 00:10:16,873
So small samples of the same steel

264
00:10:16,873 --> 00:10:19,605
are placed inside the vessel when it

265
00:10:19,605 --> 00:10:22,032
is built, and taken out over the

266
00:10:22,032 --> 00:10:23,094
years to test.

267
00:10:23,274 --> 00:10:26,048
They tell engineers how the real vessel

268
00:10:26,048 --> 00:10:26,688
is aging.

269
00:10:27,038 --> 00:10:30,105
Why do nuclear control rooms look the

270
00:10:30,105 --> 00:10:31,099
way they do,

271
00:10:31,099 --> 00:10:33,752
and why do operators train in an

272
00:10:33,752 --> 00:10:35,327
exact copy of them?

273
00:10:35,507 --> 00:10:37,771
Because of Three Mile Island.

274
00:10:37,951 --> 00:10:40,604
In March nineteen seventy nine,

275
00:10:40,604 --> 00:10:43,514
a valve stuck open and let cooling

276
00:10:43,514 --> 00:10:44,627
water escape.

277
00:10:44,807 --> 00:10:47,237
A light on the control panel showed

278
00:10:47,237 --> 00:10:49,389
that the valve had been told to

279
00:10:49,389 --> 00:10:51,610
close, not that it actually had.

280
00:10:51,790 --> 00:10:54,983
Operators believed it was closed for hours.

281
00:10:55,333 --> 00:10:57,893
Hundreds of alarms went off at once,

282
00:10:57,893 --> 00:11:00,952
and the most important information was hard

283
00:11:00,952 --> 00:11:01,521
to find.

284
00:11:01,701 --> 00:11:05,499
Investigators found that the control room design

285
00:11:05,499 --> 00:11:08,822
and operator training were major causes of

286
00:11:08,822 --> 00:11:09,851
the accident.

287
00:11:10,031 --> 00:11:13,139
After that, control rooms were redesigned to

288
00:11:13,139 --> 00:11:15,399
make critical information clear,

289
00:11:15,399 --> 00:11:17,942
and every plant in the United States

290
00:11:17,942 --> 00:11:20,273
was required to have a full scale

291
00:11:20,273 --> 00:11:20,979
simulator.

292
00:11:21,329 --> 00:11:24,577
Operators now spend weeks every year in

293
00:11:24,577 --> 00:11:29,325
these simulators, practicing accidents that will probably

294
00:11:29,325 --> 00:11:30,408
never happen.

295
00:11:30,588 --> 00:11:33,168
The simulator is an exact copy,

296
00:11:33,168 --> 00:11:35,998
down to the color of the switches.

297
00:11:36,178 --> 00:11:38,546
When something goes wrong in the real

298
00:11:38,546 --> 00:11:40,978
room, their hands already know what to

299
00:11:40,978 --> 00:11:41,170
do.

300
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:43,325
Why does a power plant,

301
00:11:43,325 --> 00:11:45,210
which makes electricity,

302
00:11:45,210 --> 00:11:47,801
need emergency diesel generators?

303
00:11:47,981 --> 00:11:50,640
Because when the reactor stops,

304
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:52,613
it cannot power itself.

305
00:11:52,793 --> 00:11:55,443
The pumps that keep cooling the fuel

306
00:11:55,443 --> 00:11:56,694
need electricity.

307
00:11:56,874 --> 00:11:59,332
If the connection to the grid is

308
00:11:59,332 --> 00:12:02,788
lost, the diesel generators must start within

309
00:12:02,788 --> 00:12:05,477
seconds and keep the pumps running.

310
00:12:05,827 --> 00:12:08,036
In March two thousand eleven,

311
00:12:08,036 --> 00:12:10,322
at Fukushima Daiichi in Japan,

312
00:12:10,322 --> 00:12:13,141
the earthquake cut the plant off from

313
00:12:13,141 --> 00:12:16,418
the grid, and the diesel generators started

314
00:12:16,418 --> 00:12:17,332
as designed.

315
00:12:17,512 --> 00:12:19,393
Then the tsunami arrived.

316
00:12:19,573 --> 00:12:22,759
It flooded the generators and the electrical

317
00:12:22,759 --> 00:12:25,076
rooms, many of them in low parts

318
00:12:25,076 --> 00:12:26,307
of the buildings.

319
00:12:26,487 --> 00:12:29,364
With no power to cool the cores,

320
00:12:29,364 --> 00:12:31,792
three reactors melted down.

321
00:12:32,143 --> 00:12:36,073
After Fukushima, plants around the world added

322
00:12:36,073 --> 00:12:37,611
more backup power,

323
00:12:37,611 --> 00:12:41,029
placed higher or in protected buildings,

324
00:12:41,029 --> 00:12:44,960
plus portable pumps and generators stored away

325
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:46,156
from the site.

326
00:12:46,336 --> 00:12:47,926
The lesson was simple.

327
00:12:48,106 --> 00:12:50,564
A backup is only a backup if

328
00:12:50,564 --> 00:12:54,074
the same disaster cannot destroy it too.

329
00:12:54,424 --> 00:12:56,550
Why can you see the cloud from

330
00:12:56,550 --> 00:12:58,676
a big power plant from tens of

331
00:12:58,676 --> 00:12:59,811
kilometers away?

332
00:12:59,991 --> 00:13:03,764
Because a large cooling tower evaporates an

333
00:13:03,764 --> 00:13:05,958
enormous amount of water.

334
00:13:06,138 --> 00:13:08,782
A big plant can release hundreds of

335
00:13:08,782 --> 00:13:11,653
liters of water every second as vapor.

336
00:13:11,833 --> 00:13:12,597
On a cold,

337
00:13:12,597 --> 00:13:15,197
humid day, the plume can rise high

338
00:13:15,197 --> 00:13:17,490
into the sky and even make its

339
00:13:17,490 --> 00:13:19,019
own clouds downwind.

340
00:13:19,370 --> 00:13:21,602
That water must be replaced,

341
00:13:21,602 --> 00:13:24,313
so plants with towers still need a

342
00:13:24,313 --> 00:13:25,987
river or lake nearby.

343
00:13:26,167 --> 00:13:28,825
They just take much less water than

344
00:13:28,825 --> 00:13:32,091
plants that cool directly with river water,

345
00:13:32,091 --> 00:13:36,268
because evaporation carries away heat very efficiently.

346
00:13:36,448 --> 00:13:39,215
Every liter that evaporates takes a large

347
00:13:39,215 --> 00:13:40,767
amount of heat with it.

348
00:13:41,117 --> 00:13:43,700
So the famous image of a nuclear

349
00:13:43,700 --> 00:13:46,445
plant is really an image of water.

350
00:13:46,625 --> 00:13:48,446
Water cooling the core,

351
00:13:48,446 --> 00:13:50,424
water shielding the fuel,

352
00:13:50,424 --> 00:13:52,481
water turning the turbine,

353
00:13:52,481 --> 00:13:55,013
and water rising into the sky as

354
00:13:55,013 --> 00:13:55,646
a cloud.

355
00:13:55,826 --> 00:13:56,976
The atom heats it.

356
00:13:57,156 --> 00:13:58,955
Everything else is plumbing.

357
00:13:59,305 --> 00:14:01,407
So here are the eleven.

358
00:14:01,587 --> 00:14:04,883
A giant tower that throws away heat,

359
00:14:04,883 --> 00:14:07,996
shaped to be both strong and thin.

360
00:14:08,176 --> 00:14:10,473
Plants with no towers at all,

361
00:14:10,473 --> 00:14:13,245
a concrete dome around the reactor,

362
00:14:13,245 --> 00:14:15,780
and rods that stop the reaction.

363
00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:18,867
A pellet the size of a fingertip,

364
00:14:18,867 --> 00:14:21,421
a pool of glowing blue water,

365
00:14:21,421 --> 00:14:23,006
a thick steel pot,

366
00:14:23,006 --> 00:14:26,617
a control room rebuilt after an accident,

367
00:14:26,617 --> 00:14:29,435
generators that must never fail,

368
00:14:29,435 --> 00:14:31,813
and a cloud you can see for

369
00:14:31,813 --> 00:14:32,342
miles.

370
00:14:32,691 --> 00:14:35,050
Next time you see those towers on

371
00:14:35,050 --> 00:14:37,623
the horizon, you will know the cloud

372
00:14:37,623 --> 00:14:38,624
is just water.

373
00:14:38,804 --> 00:14:41,793
If you want more machines explained part

374
00:14:41,793 --> 00:14:44,632
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

375
00:14:44,812 --> 00:14:46,809
And tell us in the comments which

376
00:14:46,809 --> 00:14:48,806
machine you want us to take apart

377
00:14:48,806 --> 00:14:49,108
next.
